  "query_preview": "..\nSFC moisture remains high, but PWATS have dropped substantially\ntoday. Forcing has also largely exited the region, with the \nexception of AZ/NM border. Isolated to perhaps scattered \nactivity is expected this afternoon into early evening. While \ncoverage and intensity should be less, enough moisture/instability\nand flow could produce a couple stronger storms with gusty \nwinds, small hail, and locally heavy rain. This will all be east\nof Tucson and focused near/along the AZ/NM border. It is not \nout of the question we could see some minor issues or an \nisolated and localized flash flood threat over any areas that \nhave received hvy rain over the past few days. \n\nThis weekend through Monday: Drier air finally begins to filter\ninto southeast Arizona as flow aloft shifts more westerly. This\ncould help limit thunderstorm activity somewhat and keep \nactivity mainly east and south of Tucson. High temperatures \nreturn to at or slightly above normal.\n\nMiddle to end of next week hints at moisture returning to AZ and\nperhaps daily isolated to scattered showers/storms. Most likely \narea would be south and east of Tucson, but will depend on \nthe features in play. It will be dependent on how high pressure\nevolves over Texas and any moisture possibly advecting north \nfrom Mexico. The EastPac tropics remains quite active, so\nplenty of moisture available. It will all come down to timing \nand if any larger scale troughs can pull moisture north. \nConfidence is low at this time.",
